  "name": "Send WhatsApp alerts from new LinkedIn messages with Nilyo",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "s1-main",
      "name": "Template description",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -768,
        -192
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 480,
        "height": 1270,
        "content": "## Send WhatsApp alerts from new LinkedIn messages with Nilyo\n\n### Who is it for\nAnyone who answers on LinkedIn but does not live in the LinkedIn inbox: founders, recruiters, freelancers and sales people who would rather be told on WhatsApp when a conversation moves.\n\n### How it works\nThe Nilyo Trigger subscribes to `message.new` on your own LinkedIn account and receives the event directly. The event carries identifiers, not the message body, so the workflow reads the last messages of that conversation through Nilyo before writing anything. It then sends you a WhatsApp with the sender, the new message and the tail of the thread.\n\nMessages you sent yourself, system events such as a new connection, and empty messages never raise an alert.\n\n### Setup steps\n- Create a personal token in Nilyo (Agent access) and store it in a **Nilyo API** credential.\n- Connect LinkedIn and WhatsApp in Nilyo.\n- In **Set Alert Recipient and Messages**, put the name or number of the WhatsApp contact to alert, exactly as it appears in your own WhatsApp, and how many earlier messages to include.\n- Publish the workflow: publishing registers the event subscription in Nilyo, unpublishing removes it.\n\n### Requirements\nA Nilyo account with LinkedIn and WhatsApp connected.\n\n### Customization\nReplace the WhatsApp step with Slack or email, add an LLM node to draft an answer, or switch the trigger provider to WhatsApp, Instagram or Telegram.\n\n### Community node\nThis template uses the **Nilyo** community node (`n8n-nodes-nilyo`), verified by n8n. On n8n Cloud, enable Verified Community Nodes in the Admin Panel and add Nilyo from the canvas. On self-hosted n8n, install `n8n-nodes-nilyo` from Settings > Community nodes."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-1",
      "name": "Step 1",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -208,
        -160
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 640,
        "height": 320,
        "content": "## 1. Receive and filter\n\nPublishing the workflow registers the Nilyo event destination; unpublishing removes it. The filter keeps real inbound messages only: not your own, not a system event, not empty."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-2",
      "name": "Step 2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        480,
        -176
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 416,
        "height": 320,
        "content": "## 2. Prepare the context\n\nRealtime events carry identifiers, not content, so the conversation is read through Nilyo before anything is written."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-3",
      "name": "Step 3",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        928,
        -192
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 240,
        "height": 336,
        "content": "## 3. Send the alert\n\nResolved by name from your own WhatsApp chats. Nothing is sent if several people match."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
